    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d217">

                <pb facs="0218" n="206" sameAs="frus:frus1941v01/pg206"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/352: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-09-17T14:00:00+03:00">September 17,
                            1939—2 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 4:25 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>555. Personal for the Secretary and Under Secretary. The course of events since
                    the signing of the Russian-German non-aggression pact<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d217-f1"/> culminating this morning with the invasion of Poland by
                    Russian troops apparently along the whole Polish frontier convinces me that the
                    recent understanding between Russia and Germany for the division of Poland (see
                    my telegram No. 465, August 24, noon<anchor n="3" xml:id="d217-f2"/>) has
                    reached the stage of active cooperation between the two countries. The alleged
                    pretext for the present invasion should not be permitted to hide this unholy
                        alliance.<anchor n="4" xml:id="d217-f3"/> I do not believe that Russia
                    desires or intends to take an active part in the war but in the [sharing of?]
                    the spoils which she is about to seize must of necessity do everything within
                    her power to assist her partner.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d217-f1" n="2" xml:id="d217-f1-content">
                <p>Signed at Moscow on August 23, 1939; for text, with secret additional protocol,
                    see Department of State, <hi rendition="#italic">Nazi-Soviet Relations,
                        1939–1941</hi>, p. 76; or <hi rendition="#italic">Documents on German
                        Foreign Policy, 1918–1945</hi>, Series D, Vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                        >vii</hi>, p. 245. Regarding the improvement in German-Soviet relations
                    which resulted in the conclusion of this treaty, see <ref
                        target="frus1939v01#pg_312">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, 1939, Vol. <hi
                            rendition="#small-caps">i</hi>, pp. 312</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d217-f2" n="3" xml:id="d217-f2-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1939v01#pg_342">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Ibid.</hi>, p. 342</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d217-f3" n="4" xml:id="d217-f3-content">
                <p>In regard to the note of September 17, 1939, from Vyacheslav Mikhailovich
                    Molotov, People’s Commissar for Foreign Affairs of the Soviet Union, to the
                    Polish Ambassador, Waclaw Grzybowski, asserting that the Polish State and
                    Government had ceased to exist and announcing that, in consequence, the Red army
                    had been ordered to cross the frontier to take under protection the life and
                    property of the population in the territory described as the Western Ukraine and
                    Western White Russia, see telegram No. 551, September 17, 1939, from the
                    Ambassador in the Soviet Union, <ref target="frus1933-39#pg_782">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Foreign Relations</hi>, The Soviet Union, 1933–1939,
                        p. 782</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
